      "episode_id": "VR-478073",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of INV-2207, from the case record alone (round 1):\n\nRecomputing each line (qty x unit price) against its printed line total: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. All five line totals are correct as printed.\n\nThe sum of those line items is 147.00 + 90.00 + 76.00 + 38.40 + 15.00 = 366.40. The invoice states a subtotal of 376.40, which is 10.00 more than its own line items.\n\n20% VAT on the stated subtotal (376.40) is 75.28, and 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68, so the subtotal-to-total step is internally consistent. But 20% VAT on the actual line-item sum is 73.28, and 366.40 + 73.28 = 439.68. The stated total due of 451.68 therefore exceeds the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT by 12.00.\n\nSo on the plain reading of 'line items' (the five charge rows), the claim as worded is not supported by the invoice: 451.68 does not equal 366.40 plus 20% VAT. I note the alternative reading, in which 'sum of its line items' is meant as the printed subtotal 376.40, which would make the statement true but trivial; the discrepancy between the line items and the stated subtotal would remain in either case. I will weigh the evidence actually filed before committing a verdict.",
          "content_hash": "0xd556d9bb6f6fc1461de28baade36a5ee9f1b843db94e176c37450e15a01c24ce",
          "created_at_ms": 1793325725090,
          "message_id": "3664d039-a27f-4c9a-b8d0-98b8e6f72c4e",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 independent check of INV-2207, from the case record alone (round 1).\n\nClaim tested: \"The 451.68 total due equals the sum of its line items plus 20% VAT.\"\n\nLine items, each quantity x unit price verified:\n1. Pallet wrap, 500 mm: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00\n2. Corner boards, 1.2 m: 200 x 0.45 = 90.00\n3. Strapping kit: 2 x 38.00 = 76.00\n4. Labels A6: 3 x 12.80 = 38.40\n5. Delivery charge: 1 x 15.00 = 15.00\n\nSum of line items = 366.40. Plus 20% VAT (73.28) = 439.68.\n\nThe invoice states subtotal 376.40, VAT 75.28 and total 451.68. The stated subtotal is 10.00 higher than the line items actually sum to; the stated VAT is exactly 20% of that overstated subtotal; and the stated total is 12.00 higher than 366.40 + 20%. Note 10.00 x 1.20 = 12.00, so the whole 12.00 error traces to the 10.00 subtotal overstatement.\n\nThe invoice is internally consistent with its own subtotal, but the claim is specifically about the sum of the line items, and on that basis it is not correct. I flag no uncertainty: the arithmetic is exact to the penny.",
          "content_hash": "0x08fa0462daa8c1ef1b1a447f2e8b64e6ef8e4a35e810ad91397d30679070bbcd",
          "created_at_ms": 1793325744911,
          "message_id": "d155120b-bc88-41cd-a565-61f68b1f8a1b",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 independent check of INV-2207, from the case record alone (round 1):\n\nI recomputed every line (qty x unit price) against its printed line total. All five printed line totals are correct: L1 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; L2 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; L3 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; L4 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; L5 1 x 15.00 = 15.00.\n\nSum of the line items = 147.00 + 90.00 + 76.00 + 38.40 + 15.00 = 366.40. 20% VAT on 366.40 is 73.28, so the line items plus 20% VAT come to 439.68, not the stated 451.68 - an excess of 12.00. Per-line VAT gives the same answer (176.40 + 108.00 + 91.20 + 46.08 + 18.00 = 439.68).\n\nThe invoice's printed subtotal is 376.40, which is 10.00 more than its own line items, and no 10.00 charge appears anywhere on the invoice. The subtotal-to-total step is internally consistent only in the narrow sense that 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68; the subtotal it starts from is misstated by 10.00. So 451.68 does not equal the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT under the plain reading of 'line items' (the five charge rows).\n\nI note the alternative reading, in which 'sum of its line items' is meant as the printed subtotal; that would make the statement true but trivial, and the 10.00 subtotal error would remain unexplained either way. No evidence of an unlisted charge is filed. I will weigh the evidence actually filed before committing a verdict.",
          "content_hash": "0xecb317a59285bd72a6f03bf9111d60826c0a38c76b012aa82d6967fc8350f82e",
          "created_at_ms": 1793325760587,
          "message_id": "4289d8af-0d99-4ba6-8c2c-cec4403b06e4",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Challenger's evidence for INV-2207 (Harbour Packaging Supplies, dated 2 Oct 2026):\n\nEvery individual line total is arithmetically correct:\n  1. 6 x 24.50 = 147.00\n  2. 200 x 0.45 = 90.00\n  3. 2 x 38.00 = 76.00\n  4. 3 x 12.80 = 38.40\n  5. 1 x 15.00 = 15.00\n\nBut those line totals sum to 366.40, not the 376.40 printed as the subtotal. The subtotal is overstated by 10.00.\n\nVAT and the total are then derived consistently from the inflated subtotal, so the error propagates:\n  20% of 376.40 = 75.28 (as printed), and 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68 (as printed).\n  On the actual line items: 366.40 x 1.20 = 439.68, i.e. 12.00 less than the total due.\n\nThe claim asserts that 451.68 equals the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT. On the invoice as issued it does not: the correct figure is 439.68. I record the honest caveat that the table may omit a line (a missing 10.00 charge would close the gap), but no such line appears, and the claim is about the printed document.\n\nPlease verify the five line totals and the sum independently from the case record.",
          "content_hash": "0x61d2fd3a7525bbdfd3bffc6434e260c2edaf408835f34de519840d730f5c7d95",
          "created_at_ms": 1793325764797,
          "message_id": "f9d6f54d-6198-4db2-b848-999525842550",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
      "next_after": 4
    }
